","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":74,"newStart":1,"newLines":84,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_periodic_neg_one_black_times (p N : ℕ)\n    (hc : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t)\n    (t : ℕ) (ht : N ≤ t) (hb : centerColumn t = true) :\n    evolve (t + p) (-1) = evolve t (-1) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE BLACK TIMES OF A PERIODIC CENTRE COLUMN PIN COLUMN -1, SO THE RESIDUAL OF P1 IS THE WHITE TIMES AND NOTHING ELSE. Why it is true: at a black centre cell the rule at the origin loses its middle input, so the next centre cell is the complement of the cell one to the left (centerColumn_succ_of_black). Read that at t and at t + p: a p-periodic centre column makes the two left-hand cells complements of the same bit, so they are equal. WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (open, size L, no route) becomes twelve lines with this in hand, and I have compiled that proof (explorer/seeder_scratch_p1d.lean, axioms propext and Quot.sound). The argument: suppose past some M every white time repeated column -1 too; this lemma covers the black times; so column -1 is p-periodic from max M N, column 0 is p-periodic from N, and not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1 forbids two adjacent eventually periodic columns. The same lemma is the black-time half of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times in this tier. Recommended for seeding by the theorist attack of 2026-09-08 ('the residual itself', C3: 'with the black times pinned by column_succ_of_black, the wall is a statement about column 1 at the white times of the centre column and about nothing else'); it was never seeded.",","-    "disclaims": "Does not prove P1 and does not weaken either wall. Its hypothesis is the negation of P1, so nobody can exhibit a p and N satisfying it and it carries NO WITNESS: a finite range would only confirm the hypothesis is unsatisfiable there, which is not what is claimed. It says nothing whatever about the white times, which is the entire open part. It is also not a settling or agreement-front statement and does not run into obstruction 69: it never reads a fixed bit of the packed row.",","+    "id": "rule30_alternating_step",","+    "lean_name": "rule30_alternating_step",","+    "statement": "theorem rule30_alternating_step (X : Config) (L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 2, X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0)) :\n    ∀ j < L + 1, rule30 X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The alternating row `(10)^∞` is one of rule 30's three fixed points (Wolfram 1986 Table 6.2, crystals item 22). This is that fact in the only form the rest of the tier can use: a FINITE alternating stretch running left from the origin survives one step, one cell shorter at its left end, and -- this is the part that is not just 'it is a fixed point' -- the cell AT the origin survives regardless of what sits to the right of it, because the origin is black there and the `or` in `left XOR (centre OR right)` swallows the right neighbour. So a stretch that is alternating on positions `-(L+1)..0` is alternating on `-L..0` one step later, whatever the right half-line does. Each cell is one `rule30_eq` and a Bool case split; the only friction is that the three neighbours of `-(j)` are `-(j+1)`, `-(j)`, `-(j-1)`, so the index arithmetic crosses a ℕ-to-ℤ cast.",","+    "disclaims": "Says nothing about the centre column on its own, and nothing about any prize. It is the engine of column_black_run_of_alternating and column_alternating_shrink and has no other purpose.",","     "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "have hbp : centerColumn (t + p) = true := by rw [hc t ht, hb]\nhave a := centerColumn_succ_of_black t hb\nhave b := centerColumn_succ_of_black (t + p) hbp\nhave e : centerColumn (t + p + 1) = centerColumn (t + 1) := by\n  rw [show t + p + 1 = t + 1 + p by ring]; exact hc (t + 1) (by omega)\nrw [a, b] at e\nexact Bool.not_inj e",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack"]","+      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  intro j hj\n  rw [rule30_eq]\n  have hj0 : X (-(j : ℤ)) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := h j (by omega)\n  have hjm : X (-(j : ℤ) - 1) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    have hx := h (j + 1) (by omega)\n    rw [alt_succ] at hx\n    rw [← hx]\n    congr 1\n    push_cast\n    ring\n  have hor : (X (-(j : ℤ)) || X (-(j : ℤ) + 1)) = true := by\n    cases j with\n    | zero => rw [hj0]; simp\n    | succ n =>\n        have hx := h n (by omega)\n        have he : X (-((n : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) = decide (n % 2 = 0) := by\n          rw [← hx]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n        push_cast at hj0 he ⊢\n        rw [hj0, he, alt_succ]\n        cases (decide (n % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n  rw [hjm, hor]\n  cases (decide (j % 2 = 0)) <;> simp",","+      "imports": []","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times\n    (h : ∀ p > 0, ∀ N : ℕ, (∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t) →\n        ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false → evolve (t + p) 1 = evolve t 1) :\n    ¬ IsEventuallyPeriodic centerColumn := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "A SUFFICIENT CONDITION FOR PRIZE 1, IN THE SHAPE THE WALL ASKS FOR: to prove that the centre column never repeats it is enough to control column 1 at the WHITE times of a hypothetically periodic centre column. Everything else is already pinned. Why it is true: split the times past the onset by the colour of the centre cell. At a black time, column -1 repeats for free (centerColumn_succ_of_black at t and t + p, the black-times lemma of this tier). At a white time, column_one_of_white says column 1 is the xor of the next centre cell with column -1, so the hypothesis plus the centre column's own period makes column -1 repeat there too. Column -1 is then eventually periodic and so is column 0, which not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1 forbids. WHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT: it is the model centerColumn_other_of_cohomologous_column in a second coordinate, and it converts the wall from a statement about an existential over all columns into a statement about ONE named column at a NAMED SUBSET of times. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1c.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound. By column_one_of_white the hypothesis is interchangeable with the same statement about column -1 at white times, which is exactly the negation of the open node centerColumn_periodic_damage_white; the two are one object seen from either side of the origin.",","-    "disclaims": "IT IS NOT THE WALL AND IT DOES NOT WEAKEN IT. What it buys is a smaller target, not an easier one: nothing here says the residual (column 1 at the white times) is any more tractable than P1, and I claim no evidence that it is. The hypothesis is a conditional whose antecedent is the negation of P1, so it is unsatisfiable in fact, no p and N can be exhibited, and the node carries NO WITNESS by design rather than by omission. Anyone who proves the hypothesis has proved Prize 1, which is the point and also the warning.",","+    "id": "column_black_run_of_alternating",","+    "lean_name": "column_black_run_of_alternating",","+    "statement": "theorem column_black_run_of_alternating (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n    (s : ℕ) (hs : s ≤ L) : column X 0 (t + s) = true := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "An alternating stretch of depth `L + 1` left of the origin holds the centre cell black for the next `L + 1` steps. Induction on `s`, one application of rule30_alternating_step per step: the stretch shortens by one cell a step and stays alternating, so the origin keeps reading a black cell under it, and the stretch is long enough to last `L` more steps. The direction that matters: this bounds the centre column from BELOW using only the left half of the row, so nothing about column 1 or the right half-line enters -- which is the asymmetry obstruction 9 says the black side of the centre column has and the white side does not.",","+    "disclaims": "Not a prize node on its own. It is one half of the correspondence 'black run of the centre column = depth of the alternating background'; the converse is column_alternating_of_black_run.",","     "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "rintro ⟨p, hp, N, hN⟩\nhave h1 := h p hp N hN\nrefine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1) ⟨⟨p, hp, N, ?_⟩, ⟨p, hp, N, ?_⟩⟩\n· intro n hn\n  show evolve (n + p) (-1) = evolve n (-1)\n  have e : centerColumn (n + p + 1) = centerColumn (n + 1) := by\n    rw [show n + p + 1 = n + 1 + p by ring]; exact hN (n + 1) (by omega)\n  cases hc : centerColumn n with\n  | true =>\n      have hcp : centerColumn (n + p) = true := by rw [hN n hn, hc]\n      have a := centerColumn_succ_of_black n hc\n      have b := centerColumn_succ_of_black (n + p) hcp\n      rw [a, b] at e\n      exact (Bool.not_inj e)\n  | false =>\n      have hcp : centerColumn (n + p) = false := by rw [hN n hn, hc]\n      have a : evolve n 1 = xor (centerColumn (n + 1)) (evolve n (-1)) :=\n        column_one_of_white initialConfig n hc\n      have b : evolve (n + p) 1 = xor (centerColumn (n + p + 1)) (evolve (n + p) (-1)) :=\n        column_one_of_white initialConfig (n + p) hcp\n      rw [e, h1 n hn hc, a] at b\n      revert b\n      cases centerColumn (n + 1) <;> cases evolve n (-1) <;> cases evolve (n + p) (-1) <;> simp\n· intro n hn\n  show evolve (n + p) ((-1 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((-1 : ℤ) + 1)\n  rw [show (-1 : ℤ) + 1 = 0 by ring]\n  exact hN n hn",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack", "Rule30.Proofs.ColumnOneOfWhite"]","+      "tactics": "  induction s generalizing t L with\n  | zero =>\n      have := h 0 (by omega)\n      simpa using this\n  | succ n ih =>\n      cases L with\n      | zero => omega\n      | succ M =>\n          have hstep : ∀ j < M + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n            have hs2 := rule30_alternating_step (evolveFrom X t) M (by\n              intro j hjj\n              have := h j (by omega)\n              simpa [column] using this)\n            intro j hjj\n            have hx := hs2 j hjj\n            simpa [column, evolveFrom_succ] using hx\n          have := ih (t := t + 1) (L := M) hstep (by omega)\n          simpa [Nat.add_assoc, Nat.add_comm, Nat.add_left_comm] using this",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.Rule30AlternatingStep"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 7).all (fun t => (List.range 5).all (fun j => ((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s => evolve (t + s) 0) == (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i => evolve t (-(i : ℤ)) == decide (i % 2 = 0)))))",","+      "imports": [],","+      "range": "t < 7, j < 5 -- the full iff on the seed, both directions at once"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_black_infinitely_often",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_black_infinitely_often",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_black_infinitely_often (N : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "HALF OF THE PERIOD-1 CASE OF PRIZE 1, AND IT IS PROVABLE: the centre column is never white for good. Why it is true: at a white centre cell the rule at position 1 loses its xor term and becomes an OR, so column 1 at the next step is column 1 or column 2 at this one. If the centre column were white from N on, column 1 could therefore never turn from black back to white after N, so it is monotone, so it is eventually constant, so it is eventually periodic — and so is the centre column, with period 1. Two adjacent columns cannot both be eventually periodic (not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = 0). WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (open, size L) is LITERALLY the conjunction of this node and centerColumn_white_infinitely_often, in that order — it closes as one anonymous constructor once both land. PROVED IN SCRATCH with a self-contained route needing no other node of this tier: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1e.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.",","-    "disclaims": "Nothing above period 1. There is no induction on the period here and none is available: this half turns on a monotonicity that exists only when the centre column is white at EVERY late time, and one black cell in a period destroys it. So it is not a step toward either P1 wall, and the tier does not pretend it is. A mathematician would call this easy and would very likely call it folklore; it is not in any source held here, but I did not search the literature and it should not be reported as novel. The witness is a genuine instance check rather than a shadow — every N below 8 really does get a black cell below 12 — but it says nothing about large N, where the whole claim lives.",","+    "id": "column_alternating_of_black_run",","+    "lean_name": "column_alternating_of_black_run",","+    "statement": "theorem column_alternating_of_black_run (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ s ≤ L, column X 0 (t + s) = true)\n    (j : ℕ) (hj : j ≤ L) : column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The converse: `L + 1` consecutive black centre cells FORCE the row to be alternating `1 0 1 0 ...` for `L + 1` cells left of the origin. Why: a black centre pins the next centre cell to the negation of the cell at `-1` (column_succ_of_black), so a black run makes the cell at `-1` white; then rule 30 read at `-1`, with a white cell there and a black one at `0`, is a plain negation and hands back the cell at `-2`; and so on inward, one cell per diagonal. Together with column_black_run_of_alternating this is an exact equality, not two inequalities: the length of the centre column's black run at time `t` IS the depth to which the alternating fixed point reaches left of the origin. Verified with zero mismatches over 3,960 rows of the seed and six random rows in explorer/altrun.mjs. The work is that the induction is strong: solving for the cell at `-(j+1)` needs the cell at `-(j-1)` as well as at `-(j)`, so it recurses two steps back, not one.",","+    "disclaims": "Not a prize node on its own; the pair with column_black_run_of_alternating is the vocabulary the two P1 nodes below are stated in. As mathematics this is very small -- it is two lines from Wolfram 1986's classification of rule 30's fixed points, which is already crystal 22 -- and I found no statement of it in the held sources, so call it project-internal shading into known, not novel.",","+    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "by_contra hcon\npush_neg at hcon\nhave hN : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false := by\n  intro t ht\n  have := hcon t ht\n  cases hx : centerColumn t with\n  | false => rfl\n  | true => exact absurd hx this\nhave mono : ∀ t, N ≤ t → evolve t 1 = true → evolve (t + 1) 1 = true := by\n  intro t ht hb\n  have e : evolve (t + 1) 1 = (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2) := by\n    rw [evolve_succ, rule30_eq]\n    norm_num\n    rw [show evolve t 0 = false from hN t ht]\n    simp\n  rw [e, hb]; rfl\nhave key : ∃ M, N ≤ M ∧ ∀ n ≥ M, evolve (n + 1) 1 = evolve n 1 := by\n  by_cases hex : ∃ t, N ≤ t ∧ evolve t 1 = true\n  · obtain ⟨t0, ht0, hb⟩ := hex\n    refine ⟨t0, ht0, ?_⟩\n    have all : ∀ s, evolve (t0 + s) 1 = true := by\n      intro s\n      induction s with\n      | zero => simpa using hb\n      | succ k ih =>\n          have := mono (t0 + k) (by omega) ih\n          rw [show t0 + (k + 1) = t0 + k + 1 by ring]\n          exact this\n    intro n hn\n    have e1 : evolve (n + 1) 1 = true := by\n      rw [show n + 1 = t0 + (n + 1 - t0) by omega]; exact all _\n    have e2 : evolve n 1 = true := by\n      rw [show n = t0 + (n - t0) by omega]; exact all _\n    rw [e1, e2]\n  · push_neg at hex\n    refine ⟨N, le_refl _, ?_⟩\n    intro n hn\n    have e1 : evolve (n + 1) 1 = false := Bool.not_eq_true _ |>.mp (hex (n + 1) (by omega))\n    have e2 : evolve n 1 = false := Bool.not_eq_true _ |>.mp (hex n (by omega))\n    rw [e1, e2]\nobtain ⟨M, hNM, hcol1⟩ := key\nrefine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent 0 ⟨⟨1, one_pos, M, ?_⟩, ⟨1, one_pos, M, ?_⟩⟩\n· intro n hn\n  show centerColumn (n + 1) = centerColumn n\n  rw [hN (n + 1) (by omega), hN n (by omega)]\n· intro n hn\n  show evolve (n + 1) ((0 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((0 : ℤ) + 1)\n  rw [show (0 : ℤ) + 1 = 1 by ring]\n  exact hcol1 n hn",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent"]","+      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  have aux : ∀ j : ℕ, ∀ (X : Config) (t L : ℕ),\n      (∀ s ≤ L, column X 0 (t + s) = true) → j ≤ L →\n      column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0) := by\n    intro j\n    induction j using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n    | _ j ih =>\n      match j, ih with\n      | 0, _ => intro X t L h _; simpa using h 0 (by omega)\n      | (n + 1), ih =>\n        intro X t L h hj\n        have hn : column X (-(n : ℤ)) t = decide (n % 2 = 0) :=\n          ih n (by omega) X t L h (by omega)\n        have hor : (column X (-(n : ℤ)) t || column X (-(n : ℤ) + 1) t) = true := by\n          cases n with\n          | zero => rw [hn]; simp\n          | succ m =>\n              have hm : column X (-(m : ℤ)) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) :=\n                ih m (by omega) X t L h (by omega)\n              have he : column X (-((m : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := by\n                rw [← hm]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n              push_cast at hn he ⊢\n              rw [hn, he, alt_succ]\n              cases (decide (m % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n        have hnext : column X (-(n : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (n % 2 = 0) := by\n          cases L with\n          | zero => omega\n          | succ M =>\n              refine ih n (by omega) X (t + 1) M ?_ (by omega)\n              intro s hs\n              have := h (s + 1) (by omega)\n              simpa [Nat.add_assoc, Nat.add_comm, Nat.add_left_comm] using this\n        have hstep : column X (-(n : ℤ)) (t + 1)\n            = xor (column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t)\n                (column X (-(n : ℤ)) t || column X (-(n : ℤ) + 1) t) := by\n          simp [column, evolveFrom_succ, rule30_eq]\n        rw [hnext, hor] at hstep\n        have hval : column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t = !(decide (n % 2 = 0)) := by\n          cases hb : column X (-(n : ℤ) - 1) t <;> rw [hb] at hstep <;>\n            simp at hstep <;> simp [hstep]\n        have hfin : column X (-((n : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t = !(decide (n % 2 = 0)) := by\n          rw [← hval]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n        push_cast\n        rw [hfin, alt_succ]\n  exact aux j X t L h hj",","+      "imports": []","     },","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun N => (List.range 12).any (fun t => decide (N ≤ t) && centerColumn t))",","+      "expression": "(List.range 7).all (fun t => (List.range 5).all (fun j => ((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s => evolve (t + s) 0) == (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i => evolve t (-(i : ℤ)) == decide (i % 2 = 0)))))",","       "imports": [],","-      "range": "N < 8, witness t < 12"","+      "range": "t < 7, j < 5 -- the full iff on the seed, both directions at once"","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_white_infinitely_often",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_white_infinitely_often",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_white_infinitely_often (N : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE OTHER HALF OF THE PERIOD-1 CASE OF PRIZE 1: the centre column is never black for good. Why it is true: at a black centre cell the next centre cell is the complement of the cell one to the left (centerColumn_succ_of_black). If the centre column were black from N on, that reads as 'true equals not L', so column -1 is white at every time from N on — eventually periodic with period 1 — while the centre column is black at every time from N on, also eventually periodic. Adjacent columns cannot both be eventually periodic (not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1). WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: with centerColumn_black_infinitely_often it is exactly centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (open, size L), whose statement is the conjunction of the two in the other order. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1c.lean, axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.",","-    "disclaims": "Nothing above period 1, and no induction on the period is implied or available — this half and the black half use completely different mechanisms (one the left neighbour, one a monotonicity on the right), which is why neither generalises and why the pair is not a step toward either P1 wall. Almost certainly folklore in mathematical terms; not found in the sources held here, but not searched for either, so it should not be reported as novel. The witness is a real instance check for N < 8, not a shadow, and it is silent about every N above 7.",","+    "id": "column_alternating_shrink",","+    "lean_name": "column_alternating_shrink",","+    "statement": "theorem column_alternating_shrink (X : Config) (t L : ℕ)\n    (h : ∀ j < L + 1, column X (-(j : ℤ)) t = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n    (hmax : column X (-((L : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t ≠ decide ((L + 1) % 2 = 0)) :\n    (∀ j < L, column X (-(j : ℤ)) (t + 1) = decide (j % 2 = 0))\n      ∧ column X (-(L : ℤ)) (t + 1) ≠ decide (L % 2 = 0) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The alternating region left of the origin is a COUNTDOWN: if its depth is exactly `L + 1` at time `t` then it is exactly `L` at time `t + 1` -- it cannot grow, cannot hold, and cannot shrink by two. The `≥` half is rule30_alternating_step; the sharp half is one line, because the cell that would extend the region is the negation of the first cell that broke the pattern, and negating a broken pattern breaks it again. Why this is the node the tier exists to reach: with column_black_run_of_alternating and column_alternating_of_black_run it says the centre column's black runs cannot be built up gradually. Each black run is CREATED in a single step, and it can only be created at a time when the centre cell is white -- because that is the only time the countdown is not already running. So the whole black side of the centre column is decided at its white times, which is exactly where obstruction 9 says the P1 residual lives; the two arrive at the same seam from opposite directions.",","+    "disclaims": "Proves no prize. It constrains where the hypothesis of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating could possibly be established, and does not establish it.",","+    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "by_contra hcon\npush_neg at hcon\nhave hN : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true := by\n  intro t ht\n  have := hcon t ht\n  cases hx : centerColumn t with\n  | true => rfl\n  | false => exact absurd hx this\nrefine not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1) ⟨⟨1, one_pos, N, ?_⟩, ⟨1, one_pos, N, ?_⟩⟩\n· intro n hn\n  show evolve (n + 1) (-1) = evolve n (-1)\n  have h1 : ∀ m, N ≤ m → evolve m (-1) = false := by\n    intro m hm\n    have hb := centerColumn_succ_of_black m (hN m hm)\n    rw [hN (m + 1) (by omega)] at hb\n    cases hx : evolve m (-1) with\n    | false => rfl\n    | true => rw [hx] at hb; simp at hb\n  rw [h1 (n + 1) (by omega), h1 n (by omega)]\n· intro n hn\n  show evolve (n + 1) ((-1 : ℤ) + 1) = evolve n ((-1 : ℤ) + 1)\n  rw [show (-1 : ℤ) + 1 = 0 by ring]\n  show centerColumn (n + 1) = centerColumn n\n  rw [hN (n + 1) (by omega), hN n hn]",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.NotIsEventuallyPeriodicAdjacent", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnSuccOfBlack"]","-    },","-    "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun N => (List.range 12).any (fun t => decide (N ≤ t) && !(centerColumn t)))",","-      "imports": [],","-      "range": "N < 8, witness t < 12"","+      "tactics": "  have alt_succ : ∀ j : ℕ, (decide ((j + 1) % 2 = 0)) = !(decide (j % 2 = 0)) := by\n    intro j\n    rcases Nat.mod_two_eq_zero_or_one j with hp | hp <;> simp [Nat.add_mod, hp]\n  constructor\n  · intro j hj\n    cases L with\n    | zero => omega\n    | succ M =>\n        have hs2 := rule30_alternating_step (evolveFrom X t) M (by\n          intro i hii\n          have := h i (by omega)\n          simpa [column] using this)\n        have hx := hs2 j (by omega)\n        simpa [column, evolveFrom_succ] using hx\n  · have hstep : column X (-(L : ℤ)) (t + 1)\n        = xor (column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t)\n            (column X (-(L : ℤ)) t || column X (-(L : ℤ) + 1) t) := by\n      simp [column, evolveFrom_succ, rule30_eq]\n    have hL : column X (-(L : ℤ)) t = decide (L % 2 = 0) := h L (by omega)\n    have hor : (column X (-(L : ℤ)) t || column X (-(L : ℤ) + 1) t) = true := by\n      cases L with\n      | zero => rw [hL]; simp\n      | succ m =>\n          have hm : column X (-(m : ℤ)) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := h m (by omega)\n          have he : column X (-((m : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ) + 1) t = decide (m % 2 = 0) := by\n            rw [← hm]; congr 1; push_cast; ring\n          push_cast at hL he ⊢\n          rw [hL, he, alt_succ]\n          cases (decide (m % 2 = 0)) <;> simp\n    have hmax' : column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t ≠ !(decide (L % 2 = 0)) := by\n      have he : column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t = column X (-((L : ℕ) + 1 : ℤ)) t := by\n        congr 1; push_cast; ring\n      rw [he, ← alt_succ]\n      exact hmax\n    rw [hstep, hor]\n    revert hmax'\n    cases (column X (-(L : ℤ) - 1) t) <;> cases (decide (L % 2 = 0)) <;> simp",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.Rule30AlternatingStep"]","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "column_one_succ_of_white",","-    "lean_name": "column_one_succ_of_white",","-    "statement": "theorem column_one_succ_of_white (X : Config) (t : ℕ) (h : column X 0 t = false) :\n    column X 1 (t + 1) = (column X 1 t || column X 2 t) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "COLUMN 1 CAN ONLY LOSE ITS BLACK AT A BLACK CENTRE CELL. Why it is true: the rule at position 1 is 'cell 0 xor (cell 1 or cell 2)', and a white cell 0 deletes the xor, leaving the OR. So at every white centre time column 1 moves upward and never downward. WHY THE TIER NEEDS IT: it is the mechanism of centerColumn_black_infinitely_often, and it is the forward-in-time companion of the two laws the board already has at the origin — column_succ_of_black (black centre, sideways) and column_one_of_white (white centre, sideways). Those two say what a white or black centre cell does to the column on its LEFT; this is the first that says what a white centre cell does to the column on its RIGHT, which is the column the residual of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times is about. Stated over an arbitrary configuration, like its two companions, so a half-line or damage argument can use it. PROVED IN SCRATCH: explorer/seeder_scratch_p1.lean, axioms propext and Quot.sound.",","-    "disclaims": "Unconditional and true of every configuration, so by itself it rules nothing out — it is one unfolding of rule30_eq and a mathematician would call it a definition chase, not a result. It gives monotonicity only across a white centre cell; at a black one column 1 becomes the complement of the same OR and the monotonicity is gone, which is exactly why the period-1 argument it feeds does not reach period 2. WITNESS NON-VACUITY CHECKED: the hypothesis holds at t = 2, 6, 7 in range over the seed, and the same expression with column 3 in place of column 2 evaluates to false, so the check can fail.",","+    "id": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs\n    (h : ∀ k N : ℕ, ∃ t, N ≤ t ∧ ∀ s < k, centerColumn (t + s) = true) :\n    ¬ IsEventuallyPeriodic centerColumn := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "A SUFFICIENT CONDITION FOR PRIZE 1, in the shape the wall asks for. If the centre column has arbitrarily long black runs starting arbitrarily late, it is not eventually periodic. Why: a run of length `p + 1` beginning at or after the onset `N` of a period `p` is one full period of black, so periodicity copies it forward for ever and the centre column is black from that point on -- and centerColumn_not_eventually_constant (already closed) says it is white infinitely often. The work is the strong induction that carries the run forward by whole periods; everything else is arithmetic. This is the first statement on the board that attacks P1 through the centre column's RUN structure rather than through another column, and it is what centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_deep_alternating is stated on top of.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove Prize 1: its hypothesis is unproved and is strictly STRONGER than the conclusion, so nothing can be read back from P1 to it -- Thue-Morse is aperiodic with no run longer than two. The measured black runs of the seed's centre column grow like log2 of the depth (histogram 1996, 968, 504, 257, 120, 58, 34, 16, 4, 3 over 3,960 rows, an exact coin), so the hypothesis is very probably true and no computation can establish it.",","+    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","   