hree lines from column_black_run_of_alternating (a deep alternating stretch holds the centre black that long) and centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs. Why it is worth having rather than being a restatement: the hypothesis mentions neither the centre column nor a period, so a proof of it need not reason about repetition at all -- it is a claim that a specific finite word of length k occurs at a specific place, infinitely often, and words-in-rows is the vocabulary of crystal 3 (every word has exactly four preimages) and of the leftward solve, neither of which any existing P1 block uses. With column_alternating_shrink it also says WHERE such an occurrence must be created: only at a white centre cell, which is the same seam obstruction 9 names.",","+    "disclaims": "DOES NOT PROVE PRIZE 1, and cannot be converted into a proof of it: the hypothesis is unproved and is strictly stronger than the conclusion (an aperiodic sequence may have bounded runs). It also does not survive the OR-to-XOR filter automatically -- it must be run: rule 150's centre column is constantly black, so its 'black runs' hypothesis holds trivially and its conclusion is false; the filter therefore rejects any attempt to prove the hypothesis from left-permutivity, the cone, or surjectivity alone, and the reason this node still earns its place is that its hypothesis is FALSE for rule 90 and TRUE-but-for-a-different-reason for rule 150, so it separates the three rules rather than being blind to them. No naive witness: the claim is about depths beyond any computable range, and a finite check of the hypothesis would be `unchecked` rather than a pass.",","+    "under": "centerColumn_other_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_center",","+    "route": {","+      "tactics": "  refine centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_long_black_runs ?_\n  intro k N\n  obtain ⟨t, htN, halt⟩ := h (k + 1) N\n  refine ⟨t, htN, ?_⟩\n  intro s hs\n  exact column_black_run_of_alternating initialConfig t k\n    (fun j