    "reason": "THE BLACK TIMES OF A PERIODIC CENTRE COLUMN PIN COLUMN -1, SO THE RESIDUAL OF P1 IS THE WHITE TIMES AND NOTHING ELSE. Why it is true: at a black centre cell the rule at the origin loses its middle input, so the next centre cell is the complement of the cell one to the left (centerColumn_succ_of_black). Read that at t and at t + p: a p-periodic centre column makes the two left-hand cells complements of the same bit, so they are equal. WHY AN OPEN NODE WOULD CITE IT: centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (open, size L, no route) becomes twelve lines with this in hand, and I have compiled that proof (explorer/seeder_scratch_p1d.lean, axioms propext and Quot.sound). The argument: suppose past some M every white time repeated column -1 too; this lemma covers the black times; so column -1 is p-periodic from max M N, column 0 is p-periodic from N, and not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent at i = -1 forbids two adjacent eventually periodic columns. The same lemma is the black-time half of centerColumn_not_isEventuallyPeriodic_of_white_times in this tier. Recommended for seeding by the theorist attack of 2026-09-08 ('the residual itself', C3: 'with the black times pinned by column_succ_of_black, the wall is a statement about column 1 at the white times of the centre column and about nothing else'); it was never seeded.",
